# The verdict reason names its mechanism: the red, the control and the population, Runtime flow
#
# WHAT LANDS. gen_verdict reports the EARLIEST failing line in the log. A VCS assertion prints its source
# line and its Offending line BEFORE the UVM_ERROR that names the property, so the earliest-line rule always
# quotes the least informative of the three and the reason carries a class and a position but no name. When
# the reported line has no bracketed id, the reason now takes one from the next C.MECHANISM_LOOKAHEAD lines.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS, and it is the reason this item exists. Seed 1207954461's record reads "assertion_failure at
# sim.log:45" with evidence "Offending '(!rvfi_valid)'", and sim.log:46 says sva_rvfi_irq_valid_exclusive.
# That record was read across the team as the verdict naming the wrong thing, when in fact it had named the
# EARLIEST thing and simply had not said which. The flow's selection rule was never the defect.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the verdict, `evidence`, and `evidence_line`, which is what red_expect matches, so no
# red fixture can regrade. Checked at both consumers rather than assumed: gen_verdict.grade_red_fixture reads
# evidence_line, and gen_flow_util.red_signature_check reads the verdict and the evidence.

